# Room Camera Travel and Shared-Object Continuity

Apply this rule to every Home Room → category transition and its reverse.

1. Express approach by transforming the complete Room world with one camera dolly/pan. Walls, floor, supports, neighboring props, shadows, and the clicked object must enlarge and move together.
2. Do not simulate player approach by scaling an isolated prop, destination container, screen, or full category interface out of the clicked object.
3. Aim the camera at the visible object artwork, not a broad layout anchor. Use a meaningful travel scale greater than 1 and enough duration for the environmental motion to read.
4. Keep the clicked object's silhouette, perspective, identity, and support relationship continuous at handoff. Never replace it with unrelated hardware or a different object family.
5. Mount or reveal category controls only after the camera arrives. UI may use a short local opacity/translation entrance, but it must not become the spatial motion itself and the viewport must not fade away.
6. When a category has no physical screen—especially Games/Bird—keep the object shape visible and place game-like controls around it instead of inventing a monitor or rectangular console.
7. Reverse in the opposite order: close the local UI, reconnect the same focused Room object, then restore the Room camera so the player appears to step backward.
8. Preserve one stable perspective and grounding system throughout travel. Do not independently scale grounded layers or let props drift relative to their support planes.
9. Reduced motion must skip dolly/scale movement and use an instant handoff or brief object-local dissolve.
10. Before finishing, review enter midpoint, category-active state, and reverse midpoint at desktop, 390 × 844 portrait, and 844 × 390 landscape. Reject any transition that reads as the object flying at the viewer.
